{"name":"Subtle Night Sky","hex":"#554b4f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"subtle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Senses relating to tangible things.\nOf an action or movement: very delicate or slight, and thus barely noticeable; not obvious; inconspicuous, unintrusive.\n\n Having a delicate or fine substance or texture; hence, exquisite, refined.\n Slender, thin.\n Of a substance: finely powdered; also, of particles of a substance: very fine or small.\n Of weight: after the tare (“weight of an empty container”) has been subtracted; net.","Of an action or movement: very delicate or slight, and thus barely noticeable; not obvious; inconspicuous, unintrusive."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["People or things that are subtle (adjective sense) as a class."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/subtle"},{"term":"night","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The time when the Sun is below the horizon when the sky is dark.","The period of darkness beginning at the end of evening astronomical twilight when the sun is 18 degrees below the horizon, and ending at the beginning of morning astronomical twilight."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Ellipsis of good night."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/night"},{"term":"sky","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Sikaiana."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The atmosphere above a given point, especially as visible from the surface of the Earth as the place where the sun, moon, stars, and clouds are seen.","With a descriptive word: the part of the sky which can be seen from a specific place or at a specific time; its climate, condition, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sky"}],"entries":[{"title":"Night sky","blocks":[{"text":"The night sky is the nighttime appearance of celestial objects like stars, planets, and the Moon, which are visible in a clear sky between sunset and sunrise, when the Sun is below the horizon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/%D0%A1%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%BE%D1%82_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%BD%D0%B8_-_panoramio.jpg/330px-%D0%A1%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%BE%D1%82_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%BD%D0%B8_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night_sky","wikidata":"Q1153471"},{"title":"Night","blocks":[{"text":"Night, or nighttime, is the period of darkness when the Sun is below the horizon. Daylight illuminates one side of Earth, leaving the other in darkness. The opposite of nighttime is daytime. Earth's rotation causes the appearance of sunrise and sunset. Moonlight, airglow, starlight, and light pollution dimly illuminate night. The duration of day, night, and twilight varies depending on the time of year and the observer's latitude."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg/330px-Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night","wikidata":"Q575"},{"title":"Sky","blocks":[{"text":"The sky is an unobstructed view upward from the surface of the Earth. It includes the atmosphere and outer space. It may also be considered a place between the ground and outer space, thus distinct from outer space."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg/330px-July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky","wikidata":"Q527"},{"title":"Midnight blue","blocks":[{"text":"Midnight blue is a dark shade of blue named for its resemblance to the apparently blue color of a moonlit night sky around a full moon. Midnight blue is identifiably blue to the eye in sunlight or full-spectrum light, but can appear black under certain more limited spectra sometimes found in artificial lighting. It is similar to navy, which is also a dark blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/D%C3%BCsseldorf_Midnight_view.jpg/330px-D%C3%BCsseldorf_Midnight_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midnight_blue","wikidata":"Q947638"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg/500px-Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg/1280px-Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg/500px-Starry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Milky Way is brighter in the Southern Hemisphere than in the North. (Photo taken at La Silla Observatory)[2] ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStarry_Night_at_La_Silla.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%B8.jpg/500px-%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%B8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%B8.jpg/1280px-%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%B8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/%D0%9F%D0%B